      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Brightwell Foods, a fictional company. First you're on the red team: write an email in the name of Brightwell's chief executive, Ravinder Sandhu, asking for something he would never ask for. It's the message that would land alongside a cloned-voice call to accounts payable. Then you swap seats: you're Tom Reilly in accounts payable, covering the finance office's phone and inbox, with three voicemails and two emails waiting. Only the second part is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the message you wrote, as Tom in the finance office would find it. The lines picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "It reached Tom just after nine on a Friday, in the chief executive's name, right after a voicemail in a voice just like his. By the end of the message the payment was half set up. A name and a voice you know are hard to argue with, and copying them is the whole trick; acting on it is no lapse of sense.",
        "flipDefenderLineByChoice": {
          "pretext_ceo_payment": "A voicemail in a voice like the chief executive's, then a message in his name pushing a payment 'signed off at my end' for today. By the end of it the transfer was half set up, because a name and a voice you know are hard to argue with.",
          "pretext_catchup": "A note just checking the month-end numbers asks for nothing, so there was nothing to act on. It is the request to move the money, not the familiar name, that turns a convincing impression into a loss."
        },
        "flipNoSend": "Your message didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of message reaches the finance office, and you're the one reading it.",
        "flipMissed": "The defence is the same either way: next, the same kind of message reaches the finance office, and you're the one reading it.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "Nothing in it tripped a check this time. Next, the same kind of message reaches the finance office, and you're the one reading it.",
        "noBlameLine": "A familiar voice is hard to doubt, and these calls were built to lean on exactly that trust, so anyone can be caught by one.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: a voice you recognise is not proof of who is calling, and neither is an email that backs it up. If a call or voicemail asks for money, secrecy or a skipped step, ring the person back on a number you already hold, then report it using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "A familiar voice feels like proof, and these messages were made to use that, so anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away and why ringing back on a number you already hold works whoever is speaking.",
        "defendIntro": "Go through the voicemails and emails and decide what to do with each one. Read each voicemail's transcript: what it asks you to do matters more than whose voice it is. Work through them at your own pace; it all comes back to ringing the person yourself.",
        "voicePanel": {
          "heading": "How a voice gets copied",
          "text": "Recordings of someone speaking in public, such as a talk, an interview or a company video, can be used to make new sentences in a voice that sounds like theirs. If you can't reach the person to check, ask someone who would know, such as the finance manager."
